# Protection flags for benchmark/corpus_offbynul/ -- the heap off-by-NUL ->
# overlapping program-owned records family (CWE-193 -> CWE-122).
#
# HELD IDENTICAL ACROSS EVERY TARGET IN THIS FAMILY ON PURPOSE. The only thing
# that varies between targets is WHICH HEADER FIELD the off-by-NUL corrupts and
# HOW a later legitimate operation (`trigger`) trusts that field; the menu, the
# record layout convention (header + CAP-byte data field, one contiguous pool),
# the leak channel and the off-by-one bug in set() are byte-identical across
# all six targets, including the negative control. A protection difference
# would confound that measurement.
#
# -fstack-protector-all  canary=ON. Nothing in this category touches a return
#                        address, so the canary must not be what stops the
#                        exploit -- and a family that left it off would not
#                        model the modern case.
# -pie -fPIE             PIE=ON. Makes a code-address leak MANDATORY: win()'s
#                        address is only known after show() has dumped a
#                        record and disclosed its (still-default) handler
#                        pointer.
# -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now    RELRO=FULL. There is no GOT overwrite available in
#                        this category at all -- the writable code pointer
#                        this family aims at is a program-owned record's own
#                        `handler` field on the heap, never the GOT.
# -O0                    the family's geometry IS the measurement. The header
#                        layout must stay exactly as written (a fixed-width
#                        length/capacity-like field immediately followed by a
#                        function pointer, immediately followed by a CAP-byte
#                        data array) and the off-by-one clamp
#                        (`if (n > CAP) n = CAP;`) must stay a literal
#                        comparison against the literal constant CAP -- at
#                        higher -O gcc is free to fold the clamp into the
#                        surrounding arithmetic or reorder the struct, and the
#                        target silently stops being in this category.
#
# NX is ON (default, no -z execstack).
#
# BUILT AND TESTED AGAINST
# ------------------------
#   glibc   2.35   (Ubuntu GLIBC 2.35-0ubuntu3.15)   <-- `ldd --version`
#   gcc     11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3)
#   kernel  6.8.0 x86-64, Ubuntu 22.04
#
# This category deliberately does NOT depend on any glibc allocator internals
# -- there is no chunk header anywhere in the exploited path, so unlike
# corpus_heap_variants/ or corpus_bon/ nothing here is version-sensitive with
# respect to tcache/safe-linking/aligned_OK(). The only allocator call this
# family makes at all is the single `malloc(POOL_SZ)` that creates the pool.
-fstack-protector-all
-pie
-fPIE
-Wl,-z,relro,-z,now
-O0
